Each call to a list's `GetEnumerator()` starts an independent traversal before its first element. `MoveNext()` advances that traversal; after success, `Current` supplies its element.

What does this code print, and how do the shared references and traversal positions explain the result?

```csharp
var source = new List<int> { 10, 20 };
var alias = source;

var first = source.GetEnumerator();
var second = alias.GetEnumerator();

first.MoveNext();
first.MoveNext();
second.MoveNext();

Console.WriteLine($"{first.Current}, {second.Current}");
```

Trace this code. What lines are printed, in order, and which operation causes the iterator to reach `throw`?

```csharp
IEnumerable<int> Values()
{
    Console.WriteLine("start");
    yield return 7;
    Console.WriteLine("resume");
    throw new InvalidOperationException();
}

var sequence = Values();
Console.WriteLine("created");
using var e = sequence.GetEnumerator();
Console.WriteLine("obtained");
e.MoveNext();
Console.WriteLine(e.Current);

try
{
    e.MoveNext();
}
catch (InvalidOperationException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("caught");
}
```

Trace this program for four separate runs with `mode` equal to `"normal"`, `"break"`, `"body"`, and `"iterator"`. What lines are printed, in order, in each run?

```csharp
static IEnumerable<int> Values(bool fail)
{
    try
    {
        yield return 1;
        Console.WriteLine("resume");
        if (fail)
            throw new InvalidOperationException();
        yield return 2;
    }
    finally
    {
        Console.WriteLine("cleanup");
    }
}

try
{
    foreach (int value in Values(mode == "iterator"))
    {
        Console.WriteLine(value);
        if (mode == "break")
            break;
        if (mode == "body")
            throw new InvalidOperationException();
    }
    Console.WriteLine("after");
}
catch (InvalidOperationException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("caught");
}
```
